# Vector Databases — Study Guide

> Covers embeddings, similarity search, HNSW internals, filtering, writes, and DB selection. For L1/L2 system design interviews (2-4 YOE).

---

## 1. Vectors & Similarity Basics

### Q: What is a vector/embedding and why do we need vector DBs?

An embedding is an array of numbers (typically 128–1536 dims) produced by an ML model such that **similar things end up geometrically close**. SQL handles exact matches (`WHERE id = 5`) but can't answer "find similar." Vector DBs turn "meaning" into map coordinates so "similar" becomes "nearby."

### Q: What defines "similar"?

Whatever the embedding model was trained on. General text models capture semantic meaning (OpenAI, Sentence Transformers). Custom models can capture task-specific similarity (e.g., "bought together" for recommendations — diapers and bottles aren't conceptually similar but co-purchase models place them close).

### Q: What are the common similarity metrics?

| Metric | Measures | When |
|---|---|---|
| **Cosine similarity** | Angle between vectors (ignores magnitude) | Default for normalized text embeddings |
| **Euclidean (L2)** | Straight-line distance | When magnitude matters (rare in modern embeddings) |
| **Dot product** | Cosine × magnitudes | When faster to compute, vectors pre-normalized |
| **Hamming** | Bit-differences | Binary vectors (LSH schemes) |

Interview answer: "Cosine similarity or an appropriate metric" is usually sufficient.

