## Interview One-Liners

### "How does the vector DB find similar items fast?"
> "HNSW — a multi-layer graph where vectors are nodes and edges connect nearby vectors. Search starts sparse at the top layer, greedily navigates toward the target, and drops to denser layers as it converges. Skip lists, but for high-dimensional space."

### "What similarity metric would you use?"
> "Cosine similarity — most text embeddings are normalized so we just care about direction, not magnitude."

### "Why isn't HNSW 100% accurate?"
> "It's a graph traversal — can only reach nodes connected by edges. If the true nearest neighbor isn't reachable from where we entered, we miss it silently. That's the recall trade-off."

### "How do you handle filters like 'in stock' or 'in region'?"
> "Depends on selectivity — pre-filter and brute-force for very selective filters, post-filter for loose ones. I'd benchmark with real data."

### "How do you handle writes?"
> "Vector DBs are read-heavy. I'd use a hot/cold pattern — small hot index for recent writes, big HNSW rebuilt nightly. Queries hit both and merge."

### "Which vector DB would you pick?"
> "Start with pgvector if we're on Postgres, or ES kNN if we already run Elasticsearch. Purpose-built like Pinecone or Milvus only if we're past 100M vectors."

### "What about consistency?"
> "Vector search can be eventually consistent — new items don't need to be immediately searchable."

